\name{LatticeLollipopLayerProcessor}
\alias{LatticeLollipopLayerProcessor}
\title{lattice Lollipop Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads the spikes \code{xyplot(type = "h")} draws as a \code{lollipop} layer: one
value per position, standing side by side rather than joined, as Base
R's \code{plot(type = "h")} is read. One layer per group, named after it.

Each spike is a segment from the baseline to the value; its value end is
\verb{(x0, y0)}. A spike with a missing value is not drawn, and is left out.

\code{horizontal = TRUE} draws the spikes from the left edge instead
(\code{type = "H"}), each standing at a y position with its value along x,
which is the frontend's \code{"horz"} orientation of a bar-like layer. On a
factor axis a spike's position is the level's name. A date or date-time
axis is emitted in milliseconds with a date format, as the frontend reads
a time (\code{lattice_time_milliseconds()}).
}
\keyword{internal}
